WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.050
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about</v>

2
00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:02.627
the AWS Systems manager.

3
00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:05.700
I need you to know a few things going into the exam.

4
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.190
First of all, this helps you manage your EC2 instances

5
00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.500
and on-premises servers at scale

6
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.840
because it gives you operational insights about the state

7
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.130
of your infrastructure

8
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.810
and you can easily detect programs.

9
00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.340
You can, for example, patch

10
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.490
and do patch automation at scale for enhanced compliance.

11
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.860
It works both with Linux and Windows

12
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.899
and has fully integrated AWS such as

13
00:00:24.899 --> 00:00:28.530
the CloudWatch metrics or the AWS Config service,

14
00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.150
and it's a free service.

15
00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.880
So how does systems manager work?

16
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.470
Well, we have the service

17
00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:35.550
and for the service to work

18
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.620
we need to install the SSM agent

19
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.900
onto the systems that we control.

20
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.840
So by default it's installed on Amazon Linux AMI

21
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.400
and some Ubuntu AMI.

22
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.680
Otherwise, you have to run some installation steps

23
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.110
to run the SSM agents.

24
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.930
So once it's running on your EC2 instances

25
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.600
or your on premises virtual machines or servers

26
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.680
then automatically they're going to talk to the SSM service.

27
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.010
And if you don't see the instance

28
00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.150
in the system manager service,

29
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.140
you have two problems.

30
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:04.973
Maybe it's one,

31
00:01:04.973 --> 00:01:06.690
the agent is misconfigured

32
00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:07.523
or two

33
00:01:07.523 --> 00:01:08.356
you need to make sure

34
00:01:08.356 --> 00:01:11.730
that the agent has the proper permissions to register itself

35
00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.770
with a systems manager service.

36
00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.780
So in case of an EC2 instance

37
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.760
you need to make sure you have the proper IAM role

38
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.230
attached to your instance so

39
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.780
that the SSM agent can have full permissions on SS

40
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.920
on the systems manager service to get registered to it.

41
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.860
And if we are looking at on-premises virtual machines

42
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.720
make sure the access keys are correct again

43
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.873
for the SSM agent to connect to systems manager.

44
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.930
So then once systems manager is set up,

45
00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.130
what can we do?

46
00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.240
We can run command,

47
00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.670
and run command is the ability to run a script

48
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.080
or document or just run a command across multiple instances.

49
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.080
And you can define these resource groups

50
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.230
to define how to group your instances.

51
00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.730
Then you have rate control to define

52
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.710
how fast you wanna run this command

53
00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.630
across all your instances

54
00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.420
as well as error control in case of what happens

55
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.100
if a few instances fail.

56
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.270
Do you want to fail everything

57
00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.980
or do you wanna keep on going?

58
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.840
It's fully integrated with IAM and CloudTrail

59
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.100
so no one can run commands

60
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.720
unless they have access to it.

61
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.953
And in case they run the command

62
00:02:10.953 --> 00:02:13.440
then CloudTrail will know about it right away.

63
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.180
So to run the command the beautiful thing is that

64
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.950
there is no need for SSH

65
00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.900
Only the agent will do an API call to a

66
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.500
systems manager and then will run the command for you.

67
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.670
The results are in the console.

68
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:24.750
So how does that work?

69
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.510
Well with an EC2 instance with the SSM agent,

70
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:28.530
and as you can see,

71
00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.910
the SSM agent is going to talk

72
00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.290
with a systems manager service

73
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.350
to know whether or not a run command should be issued

74
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.930
and if so while the command is run from within

75
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.643
by the SSM agents.

76
00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.480
Now a architecture that looks pretty similar

77
00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.600
is to use the send command API before

78
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.300
an auto scan group instance is terminated.

79
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.177
So the idea is that you have an ASG

80
00:02:50.177 --> 00:02:51.960
and it will terminate some instances,

81
00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.930
but then you can create a lifecycle hook

82
00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.370
to block an instance

83
00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.350
into determining wait states

84
00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.560
to perform some last commands.

85
00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:02.550
So here's an ASG

86
00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:05.670
and it terminates one of the EC2 instances

87
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:06.870
and then what's going to happen is

88
00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.670
that it's going to send an event

89
00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.490
to EventBridge called terminating wait

90
00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.350
and the instance is going to be stuck

91
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.150
in that state for a while.

92
00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.560
So once this is happening

93
00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.280
then this event from EventBridge needs to be intercepted.

94
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.380
So for this we can create a rule

95
00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.140
so that whenever we see the terminating weight type

96
00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:27.840
of event for that specific auto scaling group

97
00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.780
then we run an SSM automation document.

98
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.550
And that automation document is going to

99
00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.460
perform some actions on our EC2 instance.

100
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.290
One of them could be send command

101
00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.510
and send command would be to run the command

102
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.450
within that terminating EC2 instance.

103
00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.680
Now once the command is done,

104
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:44.513
for example,

105
00:03:44.513 --> 00:03:46.320
to extract logs or do other things

106
00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.950
then you can finally allow the auto scanning group

107
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.510
to terminate your instance

108
00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.700
by moving onto the next state

109
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.233
with the lifecycle hooks.

110
00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.440
We can also patch our instances

111
00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.030
at scale with systems manager.

112
00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.830
For this we define a patch baseline.

113
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.290
And a patch baseline is instructions

114
00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.230
on what to patch and what patches to apply.

115
00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.280
So then we have patch groups,

116
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.113
which is for example,

117
00:04:09.113 --> 00:04:10.830
we can define multiple environments.

118
00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.210
Dev, Test, Prod.

119
00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:13.200
Then maintenance windows

120
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.630
like when do we want these patches to be applied?

121
00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.230
When is it okay to maybe slow down our instances?

122
00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:23.370
And then we run the AWS RunPatchBaseline command

123
00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.200
during our maintenance window.

124
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.240
And then it is going to work cross platform

125
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.410
on Window and Linux

126
00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.900
and apply the patch to all your instances.

127
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.460
You can define rate control

128
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.700
just like the run command feature.

129
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.410
So as you can control how many instances

130
00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.640
at a time getting patched

131
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.570
and if there

132
00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.510
if there is an error threshold for the task.

133
00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.640
You can monitor the patch compliance using the

134
00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:45.690
SSM inventory to make sure

135
00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.590
that all your instances are patched and up to date.

136
00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:50.520
So here's an example.

137
00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.060
So again, we run the patch baseline task on our targets

138
00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.020
our patch groups,

139
00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.963
and this gets applied during our maintenance window.

140
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.490
Another feature is systems manager session manager.

141
00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.470
So this allows you to start a secure shell

142
00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.170
on your EC2 instances or on premises servers

143
00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.440
through the console or the CLI or the SDK.

144
00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.890
But this does not need for you to set up SSH access

145
00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:18.300
to open Port 22 or to use Bastion Host or to use SSH keys.

146
00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.290
How does it work?

147
00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.170
Well, we have an EC2 instance with the SSM agent

148
00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.840
and the proper IAM instance profile to be registered

149
00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.640
to the session manager service.

150
00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.940
And therefore we can just use the console to connect

151
00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.400
to session manager and session manager will establish

152
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.160
a connection with the SSM agents

153
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.050
so that you can run your commands directly

154
00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.910
as if you were doing SSH

155
00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.040
but you're actually not doing SSH.

156
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.250
So this supports Linux, MacOS, and Windows.

157
00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:48.120
And the idea is that the benefits of using Session Manager

158
00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.270
over SSH is that all the commands run through

159
00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.730
Session Manager are going to be logged.

160
00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.590
And that means that all that logged data

161
00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.950
can be sent to CloudWatch logs or to Amazon S3.

162
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.410
So we have full traceability

163
00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.710
of every session made onto your EC2 instances

164
00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.990
something that SSH does not give you.

165
00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.630
So then if someone runs some malicious commands

166
00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.700
you can note what they run and how they run it.

167
00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:12.600
On top of it,

168
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.820
You can use CloudTrail to intercept

169
00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.690
any start session events happening within Session Manager.

170
00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.240
Therefore you can run some kind of automation

171
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:23.010
and you can know whenever someone

172
00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:26.520
is starting to open a session onto your EC2 instances

173
00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.463
which is great for auditability and security.

174
00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:33.240
Next, we have Systems Manager OpsCenter,

175
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.070
which is a service to help you resolve

176
00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.800
any operational issues that are called OpsItems

177
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.410
related to your AWS resources.

178
00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:43.230
So OpsItems can be issues, events, and alerts.

179
00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.180
And the idea is that whenever an OpsItem

180
00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.340
is created within Systems Manager,

181
00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.950
you're going to have within only one console

182
00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.860
aggregation of information from many other services,

183
00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.560
such as any conflict changes in relationship

184
00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.990
such as CloudTrail logs, CloudWatch alarms,

185
00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.330
confirmation stack information and more,

186
00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.010
such as metrics and so on.

187
00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.440
So once you have an OpsItem with an OpsCenter,

188
00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:05.880
you have an aggregated view

189
00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.010
that should hopefully give you enough information

190
00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.110
to resolve your problems.

191
00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.460
And then on top of it,

192
00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.350
you're going to get Automation Runbooks,

193
00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.720
such as once the issue is resolved,

194
00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:17.010
or at least identified,

195
00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:20.310
you can resolve it by running an SSM automation.

196
00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.470
So how do you create these OpsItems?

197
00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:23.610
Well many different ways,

198
00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.040
but one of them is to do automation.

199
00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.500
So you can say that any time a CloudWatch alarm

200
00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.410
is being breached then create an OpsItem,

201
00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:35.460
or you can say that EventBridge can create OpsItems for you.

202
00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:38.550
So if you have any kind rules in EventBridge

203
00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:41.640
the target of them could be to create an OpsItem

204
00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:43.950
in Systems Manager.

205
00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:44.783
Okay,

206
00:07:44.783 --> 00:07:46.290
So that's it for Systems Manager.

207
00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:47.280
I hope you liked it

208
00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.230
and I will see you in the next lecture.

